task: dataflow-fan-overhead
agent: opus-dataflow
started: 2026-09-10
branch: feature/dataflow-fan-overhead
worktree: ../okay-wt-dfo2
spec: specs/dataflow.md (stage 3's own number)

why: the sink-by-sink decomposition sums to 104 ms — the source read
  once at 5, plus 13 for the route windows, 66 for the stop windows
  and 20 for the bunching — against a fan of 154 on the same feed.
  About a third of the fan's time is in none of its sinks, and the
  entry has said "decompose before optimising" since stage 3 because
  three candidates were indistinguishable from the outside.

what: separate the three, each by a lane that changes ONE of them.

  - the PRE-PASS: a fan computes one extent column per event-time
    function, so three sinks make it compute three where a lone sink
    computes one. Measured directly on `Flows.extent`, 1 vs 2 vs 3
    columns over the same source.
  - `Sink.and`'s PLUMBING: add a sink that does almost nothing (a
    count fold) to a single windowed sink. The delta is the tuple, the
    two extra virtual calls per element, and nothing else.
  - the STATE: two windowed sinks whose panes and keys are the same
    shape, against twice one of them. The delta is what having two
    operators' maps live at once costs, with the per-element plumbing
    already priced by the lane above.

  Whatever is left after those three is named as unattributed rather
  than assigned to the nearest suspect.

predict: the pre-pass is the largest of the three and the cheapest to
  fix (one pass can compute every column, and may already), and the
  plumbing is small per element but multiplied by 1.25 million. If the
  three do not add up to 50 ms I will say so and leave the entry open
  with a smaller, better-posed question rather than close it on a
  guess.
